## Issue #426 — the parse decoder had the same wall one order of magnitude out.
##
## `d_parse_diag` QUOTES the offending lexeme, so its message length is bounded by the SOURCE, not
## by anything the compiler controls. It assembled into a `strbuf(…, 1024)`; measured on the parent
## compiler it printed at most 1017 bytes and, at 1018, the diagnostic was replaced by the runtime
## abort of the compiler itself. That is the same defect the 256-byte decoders had, which is the
## argument against simply choosing a bigger number: the 1024 WAS the bigger number.
##
## The token below is a 1200-digit integer literal in a declaration-name position, so the parent's
## message is about 1312 bytes. The assertion greps the located tail rather than the quoted lexeme:
## a bare non-zero exit is also what the abort produced, and the tail is the part the abort ate.
main := fn() -> u64 {
  1
}

 := 5
